  "foaf:name": "Sustainable Aquaculture under Climate Change",
  "sdo:name": "Sustainable Aquaculture under Climate Change",
  "sdo:description": "Climate change is disrupting aquaculture, threatening food and nutrition security through reduced yields, shifting species, lower quality, and higher risks of disease and harmful algal blooms (HABs). Vulnerable regions, especially those under strong environmental variability and human pressure, are most affected. SACC will: (1) assess change patterns and ecological thresholds under multiple stressors from molecular to ecosystem scales; (2) collect and conserve genetic resources, develop new germplasm, breed climate-resilient strains, and promote healthy aquaculture; (3) refine carrying-capacity and organism–environment models and create multi-trophic, low-carbon technologies; (4) deliver data-driven guidance for industry and policy; and (5) expand knowledge exchange with youth and stakeholders, especially in SIDS and LDCs. By integrating science, governance, and capacity building, SACC will foster resilient and sustainable aquaculture futures.",
